GPU pipeline third-party
================================================================================

The backend (backend/) wraps these components to generate `.splattie` files
from photos:

LAM (Large Avatar Model) - Apache 2.0 (commercial OK)
  Source: https://github.com/aigc3d/LAM
  License: Apache 2.0 (verified in backend/vendor/LAM/LICENSE)
  Bundled as: git submodule at backend/vendor/LAM (our fork at
    https://github.com/affromero/LAM swaps the rasterizer to gsplat).
  Notes: LAM code itself is commercial-friendly, but it loads FLAME at
  runtime; FLAME's terms then apply to the combined system.

FLAME (Faces Learned with an Articulated Model and Expressions) - NON-COMMERCIAL
  Source: https://flame.is.tue.mpg.de
  License: non-commercial research license. Requires attribution.
  Bundled as: model files downloaded by backend/scripts/setup-gpu.sh
  Commercial license: contact MPI for Intelligent Systems
  (https://flame.is.tue.mpg.de) before commercial use.

================================================================================
Path to commercial use
================================================================================

You have three options for using Splattie commercially:

1. Use only the widget + format. Generate `.splattie` files through your own
   pipeline. The widget loads any `.splattie` that follows the spec - it does
   not require LAM or FLAME at runtime.

2. License FLAME commercially with MPI for Intelligent Systems. The rest of
   the stack (LAM Apache 2.0 + gsplat Apache 2.0 + Three.js MIT + Spark MIT)
   is already commercial-safe.

3. Implement an alternative head-generation method behind the
   `HeadGenerationMethod` protocol (see backend/src/splattie/methods/).
   Bring your own commercially-licensed head reconstruction model and face
   model. The rest of the stack stays the same.

The `.splattie` format itself is method-agnostic. `manifest.json.generator.method`
declares which method produced the file; the widget does not care.
